---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<Docs
  title="The schema"
  description="Item types, statuses, custom fields, milestones and saved views are declared in cairn.toml. Nothing about a workflow is built in."
  summary="Everything about a workflow is declared in one file. Nothing is built in, and the commands enforce whatever you wrote."
>
  <p>
    <code>cairn.toml</code> is the entire configuration surface. Run
    <code>cairn config</code> to see it resolved, or <code>cairn config
    --json</code> to hand it to something else.
  </p>

  <h2>Statuses</h2>

  <pre><code>[[status]]
name = "doing"
label = "in progress"
category = "active"
color = "yellow"
board = true</code></pre>

  <p>
    Order in the file is the column order on the board and the sort order in
    listings. Two properties carry meaning beyond display.
  </p>

  <h3>Category is what the program reasons about</h3>

  <p>
    Every status belongs to exactly one of <code>open</code>,
    <code>active</code>, <code>done</code> or <code>dropped</code>. Names are
    what your team says; categories are what progress bars, <code>cairn
    close</code>, default filters and checkbox rendering key off.
  </p>

  <p>
    So you can call your statuses anything. This is a real project's schema, and
    every command still works:
  </p>

  <pre><code>idea         category = open
prepping     category = open      (shown as "mise en place")
on-the-pass  category = active
served       category = done
86ed         category = dropped</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>cairn close</code> on that project produces
    <code>status: served</code>. Without the split, every feature would have to
    hardcode the word "done" and renaming a status would quietly break half the
    tool.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is also what makes import possible. Status names mean nothing across a
    project boundary — one project's <code>shipped</code> is another's
    <code>served</code> is GitHub's <code>closed</code> — so importing maps by
    category, the only axis that survives the trip.
  </p>

  <h3>Label separates what you see from what you type</h3>

  <p>
    <code>label</code> is display only. The status above shows as "in progress"
    in a table, while <code>--filter status=doing</code>, <code>--plain</code>
    and <code>--json</code> all use <code>doing</code>, so scripts keep working
    when you reword something.
  </p>

  <h2>Types</h2>

  <pre><code>[[type]]
name = "bug"
icon = "!"
color = "red"
template = """
## What happens

## Reproduction
"""</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>template</code> seeds the body of every new item of that type, so bug
    reports arrive with the same headings without anyone having to remember
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Custom fields</h2>

  <pre><code>[[field]]
name = "priority"
kind = "enum"
values = ["p0", "p1", "p2", "p3"]
default = "p2"
column = true</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>kind</code> is one of <code>text</code>, <code>enum</code>,
    <code>list</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>number</code> or
    <code>bool</code>. Enum values are ordered, so <code>--sort priority</code>
    sorts by declared severity rather than alphabetically.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>column = true</code> puts the field in tables and on board cards. A
    column that turns out to be empty for every row is dropped, so declaring a
    field you only sometimes use costs nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Milestones</h2>

  <pre><code>[[milestone]]
name = "v0.1"
title = "Usable in anger"
due = "2026-10-15"</code></pre>

  <p>
    Milestones are the sections of the rendered roadmap. Dated ones sort by
    date. An undated one keeps the position it was declared in, by taking the
    date of the next dated milestone after it — so a <code>m0-proof</code>
    written above a dated <code>m1</code> comes first, while a trailing
    <code>later</code> stays last.
  </p>

  <h2>Saved views</h2>

  <pre><code>[[view]]
name = "triage"
filter = "milestone=,category!=done"
columns = ["id", "type", "title", "created"]</code></pre>

  <p>Used as <code>cairn list --view triage</code>.</p>

  <h2>Filters</h2>

  <p>
    Every query command takes the same grammar. Clauses are combined with
    commas; alternatives within one use <code>|</code>; an empty value means
    unset.
  </p>

  <pre><code>cairn list --filter 'priority=p0|p1,category!=done'
cairn list --filter 'milestone='          # not scheduled yet
cairn list --filter 'labels~auth'         # substring or list membership
cairn list --filter 'updated&lt;2026-06-01'</code></pre>

  <p>
    Operators are <code>=</code> <code>!=</code> <code>~</code> <code>!~</code>
    <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code> <code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code>.
    Alongside your own fields there are <code>category</code>,
    <code>blocked</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>blockers</code> and
    <code>body</code> for full text.
  </p>

  <h2>Hooks</h2>

  <p>cairn does not embed a scripting language. It runs yours.</p>

  <pre><code>[hooks]
after-create = "cairn render -q"
after-change = ["python3", "scripts/notify.py"]</code></pre>

  <p>
    A string goes through the platform shell, which is convenient and therefore
    platform-specific. An array is executed directly with no shell at all, which
    is portable — use it in any project that runs on more than one system.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hooks receive the event in the environment and the complete item as JSON on
    standard input. They run after the change is on disk, so a failing hook
    warns and nothing is rolled back.
  </p>

  <p>
    The render hooks are enabled by default. Keeping the roadmap current is the
    point, so it is done rather than remembered — see
    <a href={`${base}/docs/durability`}>durability</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
